ay a move or lose. Since White has an instant win, J12 is the only option (to give myself a fighting chance, even if its a low chance).\n\nI\'m forced to admit: I believe White\'s diagonal setup is strong. It\'s almost certain that I\'ll lose. But given the goal of finding a "winning move", and since there\'s no way to block White from winning immediately, I\'m choosing J12.\n\nFinal answer: J12.\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: The current board shows that Black has a strong diagonal line of three stones at G9, H10, and I11. Extending this line is Black\'s most promising strategy. White has a very dangerous configuration on the diagonal E9-F10-G11-H12, which forms a "Live Four" (four consecutive stones with open ends at D8 and I13). This is typically a winning state for White on the next turn. However, since it is Black\'s turn, Black must attempt to win immediately or force a defensive sequence. Blocking only one end of White\'s line (D8 or I13) would leave the other open for White to win. Thus, Black\'s best chance is to play an offensive move that creates a "Live Four" of their own, forcing White to respond, or hoping to win the race if White\'s threat is not realized.\n\nReason: Playing at J12 extends Black\'s diagonal line (G9-H10-I11) to create a "Live Four" (G9-H10-I11-J12). This threat forces the opponent to block at either F8 or K13 to prevent Black from forming 5-in-a-row on the next move. While White has a lethal threat, Black\'s only winning attempt is to seize the initiative with this forcing move.\n\nAnswer: {"col": "J", "row": 12}'}
